hen someone calls  my  one for an overdose that call is does  not go to a police department it only  goes to fire and ambulance which the  idea is hopefully removes a degree of  hesitation that some of you drug users  might have to call 9-1-1  yeah and and that gets to the the sort  of discussion around decriminalization  versus legalization and you know in the  United States when it comes to marijuana  there's sort of a patchwork of laws  depending on the state that you're in of  course marijuana is still illegal at the  federal level and there's this big  debate about decriminalization as a good  first step towards legalization versus  the idea that these are sort of two  really separate paths with very very  different implications can you talk a  little bit about that I mean it  practically speaking why is the  difference between decriminalization and  legalization so important yeah I mean  that's I'm so happy I asked that  question because I think it is one of  the most important questions that we  have in this debate on drug policy in  the context of the crisis right now  decriminalization deals with the demand  side so all decriminalization does is  remove criminal laws that make it  illegal people the whole drugs for  personal possession legalization goes  the next step it's much more complicated  it involves fully legalizing and  regulating substances sort of some  possibly similar to how we regulate  tobacco and alcohol  so the idea advocates for  decriminalization say would remove  stigma would bring drug use to be open  it would help it would encourage people  to seek help seek treatment but it would  not solve the fentanyl crisis that we  talked about earlier decriminalization  would leave supply in the hands of  dealers an organized crime whereas  legalization the next step would remove  drug supply from organized crime and  bring it under the control of government  right so even in cases where we've seen  legalization like for example in  Colorado in the United States with  marijuana it doesn't eliminate the black  market but it significantly reduces it  does decriminalization do anything at  all to deal with the black market aspect  of the supply it really doesn't and  while I'm an advocate for  decriminalization this is why I think we  have to be cautious about it it's a  first  step in my opinion but it would not  solve the fentanyl crisis supply would  remain in the hands of organized crime  and organized crime would continue to  poison really poison these drugs with  fentanyl what about the public sort of  stigma I guess or let me rephrase it a  different way do you have any strong  feeling as to whether legalization more  drastically changes the public  perspective on a particular substance  compared to just decriminalization yeah  again I think the criminalization is an  important first step because I do  believe it would learn it would go a  long way in changing public opinions  towards drug use if drug use is no  longer a criminal matter we can we can  truly let it become a healthcare matter  and encourage people to speak treatment  speak about and more openly because it  won't be criminal anymore um  legalization would continue that process  they would sort of normalize normalized  drug use and I mean that in a positive  way I it would allow people to use drugs  out in the open I'm not using drugs  alone where if they overdose there's no  one to catch them if they they overdose  and it would it would allow us to  control how how the public perceives  drugs for example a lot of jurisdictions  have a large warning labels on packages  of cigarettes we can do the same sort of  thing with dangerous drugs as opposed to  leaving market in the in the hands of  organized crime areas now can you tell  us more about what's been done in  Vancouver you already talked about how  when you call emergency services for  possible or actual overdose in Vancouver  police don't respond you basically have  ambulance and and firefighters and that  it's really dealt with as a health  incident not as a criminal incident what  else has been done in Vancouver yeah so  first to give people an idea of the  scale of the problem here Vancouver's  over overdose crisis is about on par  with Philadelphia's is a good comparison  but 50 overdose deaths per hundred  thousand people is the rate and then  that is an astronomical rate of  overdoses it's something really scary in  response and the government has taken  some really radical measures mostly  since the winter of 2016 so when you  call 9-1-1 police don't show up call  only goes to medical and fire we have  essentially flooded the streets with the  overdose antidote naloxone and it's free  you get it over the counter in  pharmacies you walk down the street in  Vancouver you see it clip the people's  belts it's really everywhere we've also  opened a ton of new sort of low barriers  supervised injection sites places where  people can bring bring illegal drugs  mostly heroin cocaine and  methamphetamine and used them under the  watchful care of staff we can respond in  the event of an overdose Vancouver of  course established North America's first  supervised injection site in 2003 we  only had that one site for the entire  next decade since the winter of 2016 our  city alone is open more than half a  dozen additional sites and they're  reversing overdoses every single day so  there are people who will hear you talk  about you know supervised places to use  for recreational drug use and they'll  say that in the net will increase the  number of people engaging in risky  behavior with drugs what evidence do we  have to push back against that talking  point yeah and and I do understand that  talking point there is logic behind it  but the data does not actually tell us  that's what occurs in sight at North  America's first supervised injection  site in Vancouver since 2003 is very  possibly and the most studied health  intervention in our continents 